Activity 10 - Deploying MySQL in Kubernetes Cluster with Secret and Persistent Volumes



Refer the link, to access the MySQL docker image description on the Docker public registry. As described, it's mandatory to provide the MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD environment variable when creating a MySQL container.

Reference Link - MySQL Image

To pass value to the MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD environment variable, we will use a secret object. There are two ways to achieve this:


# first convert the text into base64 encoding using the command
echo -n "YOUR-PASSWORD" | base64; echo


i. The imperative way -

kubectl create secret generic mysql-secret --from-literal=password=<YOUR-BASE64-ENCODED-VALUE> -n development


ii. The declarative way -
Move to the my-db directory in the lab-files directory, and refer the file mysql-secret.yml.

cd ~/container-orchestration-with-kubernetes/lab-files/my-db
vim mysql-secret.yml


NOTE: The Secrets store data in the base64 format, whereas the ConfigMaps store data in a plain text. For sensitive data like keys, passwords, database connection string etc. always use the Secrets.

apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: mysql-secret
  namespace: development
  labels:
    app: mysql
data:
  password: <YOUR-BASE64-ENCODED-VALUE> # REPLACE HERE



To check the secret object created use -


# gets all the secret object in development namespace
kubectl get secret -n development
# describe a specific secret object, in this case mysql-secret
kubectl describe secret mysql-secret -n development



A PersistentVolume (PV) is a piece of storage in the cluster that has been provisioned by an administrator. It is a cluster resource. PVs are volume plugins like Volumes, but have a lifecycle independent of any individual pod that uses the PV. To create a new PersistentVolume with the name of mysql-pv of type hostPath apply the configuration given in mysql-pv.yml file -


kubectl apply -f mysql-pv.yml



To check the persistent volume use -


kubectl get persistentvolumes
# OR
kubectl get pv


Reference Link - Volumes
Reference Link - Plugins
Reference Link - Access Modes
Reference Link - hostPath

A PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) is a request for storage by a user. It is similar to a Pod. Pods consume node resources and PVCs consume PV resources. To create a new PersistentVolumeClaim with the name of mysql-pvc apply the configuration given in mysql-pvc.yml file -


kubectl apply -f mysql-pvc.yml



To check the persistent volume claim created use -


kubectl get persistentvolumeclaims -n development
# OR
kubectl get pvc -n development



To launch a new mysql pod refer file mysql-pod.yml. To apply the configuration use -


kubectl apply -f mysql-pod.yml



To apply a watch, use the -w option that will display the state change based on different events.


kubectl get pods -n development -w


NOTE: Press Ctrl + C / Control + C to come out of the watch.

To exec into the mysql-pod use -


kubectl exec -it mysql-pod -n development -- bash



Now, to access the mysql server use -


mysql -u root -p




